\p{Sums over the 56 rows (42 official accepts, 14 official rejects). Candidate, plain run: 17 rejections of official accepts, 8 accepts of official rejects. Published build, plain run: 16 and 7. Candidate, machine alone: 17 and 8, and 1 decline. Base, machine alone: 7 declines, each of them decided with the official verdict by the reference engine in a plain run. Candidate with every declaration in the mode: 15 rejections of official accepts, 5 accepts of official rejects, 2 declines. The 9 different rows that some build accepts against the official verdict all state a derivable proposition: five state \code{True}, and for \code{rv2a-pi-proj-let-escaped-bad}, \code{rv2a-proj-struct-closure-var-bad}, \code{rv2a-chain-typed-binding-closure-bad} and \code{rv2a-escape-proj-far-frame-bad} the hypothesis \code{W} applied to a constructor proves the statement.}
}

\subtree{
\title{8. Why no accept of a statement that is not derivable was found}

\p{Section 7.1 of the report of MO-2 says that firing the K rule under any valid type of the major premise is derivable. The review checked the other half: that every type the mode uses \em{is} a type of that major premise. \code{Infer.outType} computes each state type from the value in the major position by the machine's own VTYPE and INFER: the declared type of a let record, INFER of its value term in infer-only mode, the type of a kept application from the type of its head value, a projection through the type of its struct. None of these reads a type of another value: the memo words they use (\code{M_TYPE} of a kept application, of a let record, of a typed binding) are functions of the record's identity, and the state types beyond state 0 are not stored. The wrong verdicts of sections 3 to 5 are all of the same form: the mode picks, among the valid types of the major premise, one that the official kernel does not have at that moment. That changes whether the rule fires; it cannot make the replacement of a proof by a constructor underivable.}

\p{Where a wrong reading could have gone further, it did not. Two binders that are one record (F1) have the same value term and the same declared type, so the confusion is between two valid types of one value. The marks overwrite memo word 1 of a let record only where it is not \code{LET_EXACT}. A let record bound in infer-only mode never gets \code{LET_EXACT} and counts as having two types, which adds states and can only add declines. This is an argument from reading about 1,600 lines once; no export was written that tries to break the typing judgments themselves, which the patch does not touch.}
}

\subtree{
\title{9. Attacked without result}

\ul{
\li{\strong{A typed binding on the way of the chain, after the redex} (the second caveat of section 7.3 of the report of MO-2): \code{rv2a-chain-typed-binding-var-ok} and its mirror, in which the comparison runs after the redex and inside an outer let whose variable is the argument, with a binder domain that would give the other answer. Official verdict on both. The reviewer's prediction of a wrong reject was wrong. With the five exports of the author that makes seven; still not an argument. The typed binding fails where the comparison belongs to the typing of the body (F2, F3), not where it belongs to the type that results.}
\li{\strong{The reduction of the recursor found in a memo, so that no K comparison is made}: an earlier declaration, or an earlier part of the same one, reduces \code{\verb~|Eq.rec 7 ax~}; then a let variable is applied and forced without the K rule; then \code{\verb~|Eq.rec 7 (f Unit.unit)~} is evaluated. Three exports (\code{rv2a-memo-*}), official verdict on all: the K comparison is made all the same and starts the mode (\code{let.restarts} 1).}
\li{\strong{Proof irrelevance on a stuck projection in the first run}: of a let variable inside and after its scope, of a beta-redex (six exports \code{rv2a-pi-proj-*}). The default switch set gives the official verdict on five and accepts the sixth, a derivable statement. The generic tier does not (F12).}
\li{\strong{A projection of a let variable whose value is a constructor application}, inside the scope and after it (\code{rv2a-krule-major-proj-let-ctor-*}): official verdict from the candidate; the published build rejects the second.}
\li{\strong{The marks and the two scope declines} (item 5 of the author's list), by reading only: a value that holds a marked variable is reached through the frame of a closure or a delayed term, the type of a free variable, a spine or a struct, and \code{letMentions} visits all of them; a memo word of a record that does not hold the variable cannot hold a value that does, except the stored type of a tabling record, which is the type the let chain returns. No export breaks it. The one hole found is F1, which is about what "the variable" is.}
\li{\strong{The lambda-body rule} (item 1), by reading: where \code{lamBodyRoot} cannot read the root it takes every variable of the frame, and a body rooted at its own binder is typed by the domain in every state. Not broken. What fails next to it is the answer the rule keeps: a delayed major premise without a stored type is typed by its forced value (F10).}
\li{\strong{The stored type} (item 4): no stored type was found that is used past a let variable which the frame walk does not see.}
\li{\strong{A let whose value is a recursor application, a let inside the value of a let}: read, not exported; the chain ends at such a value term and the state type is INFER of the term, which is the official reading.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{10. Not attacked, and errors of the reviewer}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Budgets} (item 9). The one export written for the bound of 16 on the chain, \code{rv2a-chain-17-lets-ok}, is void for a plain run: its two declared types are one value for the machine, nothing starts the mode. Forced into the mode it is declined ("the type root of the major premise lies too deep"), which is the bound doing what it says. No real unit was looked for.}
\li{\strong{Structure eta, the unit-like rule and eta expansion in the mode.} F8 and F12 show proof irrelevance; the other sites type the same structs and were not exported.}
\li{\strong{Eager reduction inside the mode}, universe-polymorphic K types beyond the author's rows, the over-application rule on the six real units, the warm session path of section 7.3 for a later declaration that never binds the variable.}
\li{\strong{The gates and the guard of the report of MO-2 were not repeated.} Three corpora were run once through an instrumented private copy of the candidate for the counts of section 11 (Std, cslib, Mathlib: accepted).}
\li{\strong{The x86-64 architecture.}}
\li{\strong{Predictions that were wrong}: the typed binding in the chain (section 9), the memo exports (section 9), and proof irrelevance on a stuck projection of a let variable in the first run, which the reviewer expected to be a wrong reject of the published default and which the default switch set accepts.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{11. A second question: is there a simpler rule in the ordinary tier?}

\p{\strong{This section is an opinion with reasons, not a finding. Nothing of it was built.} The question: could one rule in the machine's ordinary tier give the official answer for the whole class (let variable, let term, beta-redex, over-application, projection of a constructor as the major premise) instead of a second run, a scope stack and four declines?}

\subtree{
\title{11.1 One root, four places where it shows}

\p{Every finding of the report of MO-2 and of this review, the scope half aside, is one fact: the official kernel types a \em{term} and the machine types a \em{value}, and the value has forgotten a step. It forgets in four places. (1) A delayed argument is replaced by its value once that is known (PEEK, \code{Values.mkSusp}), so a redex, a let term or a projection arrives as its reduct. (2) The type of a delayed term without a stored type is the type of its forced value (\code{Infer.vtypeSusp}), and FORCE runs through let variables. (3) A let variable is one record for every binder with the same value term, frame and declared type, on both sides of its scope. (4) The type of a lambda is "the type of the body under the argument", computed again per argument, so a comparison that the official kernel made once under the binder is made again under the value (F2, F3).}
}

\subtree{
\title{11.2 The rule}

\p{\strong{A delayed argument is typed as the term it is, and stays the term it is.} In two parts.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Typing.} VTYPE of a delayed term is INFER of its term under its frame in infer-only mode, never VTYPE of FORCE, and the result is the stored type of the tabling record. That is the official \code{infer_type} on the official term: a redex through its body under a typed binding of the domain, a let term through its body under a let record of the declared type, a projection of a constructor through the parameters, an over-application through the body of the lambda, the application of a let variable through the declared type. The judgment exists; the generic tier uses it wherever INFER stored a type, and with every declaration in that tier the candidate gives the official verdict on F5, F6, F9 and F11 of this review. The author proposes the same step for MO2-F3 and MO2-F6.}
\li{\strong{Keeping.} A delayed term whose type root is not inert is never replaced by its value in a spine or a frame: a redex, a let term, a projection, or an application whose head variable is bound to a closure, a let record or another such term. That is the class for which ARGV already makes a suspension instead of a neutral value (\code{inertHead}); the change is that the suspension is handed on instead of its value. Without this part the first part is exact only for a major premise that nobody forced before, and the answer depends on the order of evaluation.}
}

\p{With both parts the kept application, \code{plainRoot}, the lambda-body rule, \code{letLost} and the trigger's list of shapes are not needed: F4, F7 and F10 cannot arise, because nothing is evaluated to find a type root.}
}

\subtree{
\title{11.3 What it costs}

\p{\strong{Per K comparison: one infer-only inference of the major premise, memoised on its tabling record}, a walk over the spine with one INST per argument, the same order as VTYPE of the neutral value. And there are few of them. Counted with an instrumented private copy of the candidate (the JVM launcher, 8 workers, no option, one pass, the aarch64 host; a K typing is counted when the K rule computes the type of its major premise, not when the reduction is found in a memo; second runs in the mode included):}

\table{
\tr{\th{Corpus}\th{K typings computed}\th{Declarations with at least one}\th{Declarations the machine started}\th{Major premise a neutral application}\th{a free variable}\th{a projection value}\th{a let variable, two types / one type}\th{a kept application}\th{a delayed redex}\th{a delayed let term}\th{a delayed projection}\th{a delayed application of a variable}}
\tr{\td{Std}\td{4,076}\td{889}\td{91,711}\td{3,321}\td{751}\td{0}\td{0 / 3}\td{0}\td{1}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{cslib}\td{8,370}\td{2,963}\td{368,173}\td{8,289}\td{59}\td{11}\td{0 / 3}\td{3}\td{4}\td{1}\td{0}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib}\td{46,376}\td{8,247}\td{656,753}\td{44,442}\td{1,523}\td{326}\td{5 / 24}\td{8}\td{23}\td{12}\td{1}\td{3}}
}

\p{On Mathlib 9 more are constants. Of the 39 delayed major premises of Mathlib, 9 have a stored type, 23 are not yet forced and 7 are forced already (their value is known and their type is still asked through the suspension). So the K rule meets a delayed redex, let term or projection 36 times on Mathlib (39 with the delayed applications of a variable), 5 times on cslib and once on Std: these are real inputs of the shape of findings MO2-F3 and MO2-F6; those without a stored type are typed today by the type of the reduct, with no verdict known to depend on it. The typing part of the rule is free.}

\p{\strong{The cost is in the keeping part, and it was not measured.} A value that holds a suspension where it held a value is another record: fewer identical records, fewer hits of the memo words and of the pair table, more work in the structural comparison, which must look through the suspension. How many of the neutral major premises above are reducts that a kept term would have replaced cannot be read off the value; that needs a count at ARGV. The mode's own counter gives one bound from below: 172,023 kept applications of let variables on the eight corpora when every declaration runs in the mode (the report of MO-2, section 8.4), and redexes are more frequent than applied let variables: every \code{have} of a real proof is one. The earlier experiment with let values that are never replaced (erratum E1 of the reconciliation, the notes of package M2) ended in non-termination; this rule is narrower (a kept term is still forced once and its value memoised, only its identity is kept), but that experiment is the reason to measure before believing.}
}

\subtree{
\title{11.4 What it does not cover}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The scope half} (finding MO2-F1, and F1 of this review). It is not a typing rule: after \code{infer_let} the official variable does not exist. The official kernel abstracts the variable out of the returned type and substitutes the value term when the type is reduced. The machine can do the same and nothing less will be exact: a let record per \em{binding} (a serial number in its identity, so that two binders are two records), and at the end of the let chain, if the returned type holds the record, a rewrite of that type with the record replaced by the delayed value term, which the rule of 11.2 then types as a term. That removes the states, the marks and the scope stack. It costs a traversal of the returned type for each let whose type holds its variable, and it ends the sharing of let records between a lambda body and its later typings under an argument. Not measured. The alternative is the comparison in every state that the patch has, with a record per binding to repair F1.}
\li{\strong{The typing of a lambda body again under the argument} (F2, F3). The official kernel computes the type of a lambda once, under the binder, and instantiates the result. A machine that computes it again per argument replays every reduction that the typing of the body needs, with the argument where the binder was. An argument that keeps the domain as its type when it is passed on, as the typed binding does for INFER of the variable only, would cover the K rule on the binder; it puts a record into values that is not the value. No simpler exact rule is seen here.}
\li{\strong{The shortcuts of the default set} that build or compare values without the term (code blocks, the lazy evaluation of one side, the proof-comparison shortcuts): the rule changes the default tier, so each of them has to be shown to hand the kept term on. That is a change of every declaration's values, with all gates and an adversarial pass of its own.}
}

\p{\strong{In short.} The class has a simple exact rule for typing, and it is cheap where the K rule runs. What is not simple is what the machine gave up for speed: a value that is its own normal form, a let variable that is shared, a lambda type that is recomputed. A second run in a mode is a way to pay those prices only for the few declarations that need it, and that idea is sound; what failed in this build is that the mode still works on values (kept applications found by evaluation, guards on terms whose variables are bound to values) and that it is another tier than the one the declaration was trusted in. A mode built on the rule of 11.2 plus the substitution at scope exit, started by a flag that says "a non-inert delayed term was replaced by its value, or forced through a let variable, in this declaration" instead of by a list of shapes, would not need the chain, the states or the declines. How many declarations that flag would run twice is the number to measure first.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{12. What would change the verdict, and rows for the suite}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The six wrong rejects of section 3 are rejections of the second run.} On the eight corpora every one of the 93 runs in the mode is an accept (the report of MO-2, section 8.4). A second run that may accept and may not reject, with a rejection in the mode treated as "no verdict" as the base treats the whole unit today, would keep the 80 real units and turn F3, F4, F7, F8, F12 and the mode's part of F1 and F10 from wrong rejects into declines. It would leave the two new accepts of derivable statements (the mirrors of F4 and F7) and the trigger holes of section 5, which the published build has too. This was not built and not tested; it is the smallest change the findings suggest, not a repair of the mode.}
\li{\strong{55 rows are proposed for the hostile suite} (all exports of section 7 but the void one), with the official verdicts, in the format of the manifest: 9 with the kind T and the reason in the text; 16 official accepts that a plain run of the published build rejects, which the gate cannot take today because it lets the count of known wrong rejects go down only; 30 strict rows. Six of the strict rows are the wrong rejects of the candidate: with them in the suite the gate of the candidate fails, which is what a gate is for.}
\li{\strong{For the argument of section 7.2 of the report of MO-2}: step 1 ("the value in the major position is the official term") fails for a delayed term typed by its forced value and for a struct or a value term whose variables are bound to closures; step 3 ("the scope stack is the official local context") fails for two binders that are one record; step 4 ("everything else is the generic tier") is true and is itself a source of wrong rejects; step 5 ("the mode is reached") fails for four shapes.}
}
}
